ant  things and yes um obviously i would like  to see more important things talked  about we've had a real successful  rollout of the vaccine we've got one of  the fastest growing economies fastest  growing in the g7 in fact as we come out  of this pandemic  yes we've got uh issues to deal with as  we come out of the pandemic but this is  a government that's really getting on  with those real challenges and here in  glasgow we're actually convening the  world to tackle a global challenge and  those are the issues that i've been  focused on this week  all right look i'm going to come to that  in just a second but let me take the  other part of john mage's attack which  is not specifically to do the  conservative party but politics itself  you know what's happened in the last  couple of months you've raised people's  taxes  petrol prices are up  mortgages the supermarkets shelves uh  are uh to some extent have been cleared  over the last couple of months  surely  people are right to say  all of that is going on and there they  are and this is all members of  parliament talking about themselves  again and this is not made up by the  media  this was parliament's parliamentary  business  it was set by  you and your colleagues and of course  the opposition  do you see that there's a problem here  well look parliament obviously deals  with all sorts of agendas across the  week recently i introduced a new bill  that's going to protect animal welfare  and ban the live export of animals which  is a manifesto commitment the  environment bill is going through  parliament at the moment that's going to  introduce due diligence in supply chains  to  you know make sure that forest risk  products like palm oil are sustainably  sustainably produced where organisms are  going to have a whole set of  environmental targets set under that  that's just on my uh brief and there'll  be other legislation and important  business of government going through  parliament at all times this is one  motion uh on an appeals process um  obviously yes of course it's attracted  more attention than some of those other  more substantive more important pieces  of legislation going through parliament  but it doesn't detract from the fact  that those are more important and those  are the issues that are taking up  government time  all right well let's turn to a big  substantive issue which is going on  where you are and that is uh cop 26.  um  gretel thunder tundberg has said yet  again it's blah blah blah photo  opportunities for leaders um you don't  have to go all the way with her  to  accept that there might be a problem you  signed a coal deal this week and that's  a big thing  but australia hasn't signed it the usa  hasn't signed it and china responsible  for about half the world's coal emission  hasn't signed it what is the point of  that  well some of those countries i think  china and the u.s in particular have  signed uh separate agreements i think  they were reached at g20 on ending  investments overseas on unabated coal so  that's a step forward always in these  multilateral agreements  different countries have got  different uh concerns  different circumstances you're never  going to be able to get everybody to  move the important thing is are you  overall moving the world forward and i  think to get over 100 countries  committed to phasing out coals a major  step forward  and i also think that if you look at the  fact that we've now got 90 yeah yeah but  we've got 90. that is the point overall  um well overall and you haven't got the  biggest contributors the head of the um  international energy agency faith barol  says that in the absence of those  countries australia china and so on the  target of keeping temperature at no more  than 1.5 percent above pre-industrial  levels the chances of doing that are  quote close to zero  close to zero  well we don't know obviously with the  measures that uh that are agreed once uh  cop concludes in a week from now 